            <ArticleTitle>A case of choroidal neovascular membrane in 6-year-old boy with juvenile idiopathic arthritis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>18</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1186/s12348-017-0136-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this study is to report a case of choroidal neovascular membrane (CNVM) in a patient of juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">The design of this study is an interventional case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 6-year-old boy, a known patient of JIA, presented with a complaint of redness and ocular pain with mild diminution of vision in his left eye. Fundus examination revealed a CNVM with retinal thickening and subretinal fluid which was confirmed on OCT. Treatment with intravitreal injection of anti-VEGF (ranibizumab) and oral immunosuppressive showed resolution of the CNVM.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The result of this study is a successful treatment of CNVM with a single anti-VEGF injection and systemic immunosuppression.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Although a rare complication, CNVM can occur in patients with JIA.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
